{-
   The raw pointer, and the two operations that are places rather than calls.

   `lib/Native/Pointer.yana`'s own two declarations, cut down to what `compiler/resolve_mir`
   compiles. `*p` is a read of the object the pointer names and `store(p, ->v)` is a write of it;
   what the compiler supplies is the node, and the signature here is the signature - see
   `compiler/intrinsic/op.def`.

   `store`'s second parameter is `->value` because the pointee holds the value afterwards, so the
   frame that owned it stops owing it a teardown. `op.def`'s shape says so, and a declaration that
   wrote a plain parameter there is refused where it is written.
-}

@builtin(pointerLoad)
pub fn load(it: %a) -> a

@builtin(pointerStore)
pub fn store(to: %a, ->value: a) -> {}

{-
   The address of a value, and where a pointer's provenance is stated.

   **The parameter publishes an origin and the result names it.** That is not decoration and it is
   not the same declaration `lib/Native/Pointer.yana` currently carries: written `it: a` the position
   is an ordinary unpublished one, so a scalar argument is *copied* into the call and what comes back
   is the address of the copy - the wrong storage, and an address the origin environment finds rooted
   in nothing. Written this way the storage `it` names stays live to the pointer's last use, which is
   Analysis-Borrows-Revised.md §4.1, and §12.3(c)'s refusal has a relationship to check.

   The tick is on the **pointee**: `%source'a` is a pointer *into* `source`'s storage, where
   `source'(%a)` would be a pointer read *through* a borrow of somebody's record. Both mean
   something and they are not the same thing.
-}
@builtin(addressOf)
pub fn addressOf(it: source'a) -> %source'a

{-
   A borrow of what a pointer names - the one operation that re-enters the checked world from the
   unchecked one.

   Nothing here verified that the pointer is valid, that what it names is initialized, or that
   nothing else writes through it while the borrow is live, which is exactly what makes it Native's
   and not the language's. What the signature does promise is where the result is rooted, and that is
   what gives the borrow the caller's extent instead of the call's.
-}
@builtin(pointerBorrow)
pub fn borrow(it: %source'a) -> source'a
@builtin(pointerBorrowMut)
pub fn borrowMut(it: %source'a) -> &source'a

{-
   Arithmetic in **elements** rather than in bytes: `p + 1` advances by one `a`, whatever an `a` is.
   Byte-granular work casts to `%U8` first, where the two coincide.

   The scale is the element's *stride* and not its size - a record's trailing padding belongs to
   whoever contains it, so two `data P {a: I64, b: U8}` are sixteen bytes apart where one value of it
   is nine, and arithmetic in sizes overlaps its own elements.

   Written as ordinary names here rather than as the operators `lib/Native` declares, because a role
   is bound by `@builtin` and the compiler holds no name for any of these - the operator syntax is a
   separate thing, and what this file is is the declarations the compiler implements.
-}
@builtin(pointerAdd)
pub fn advance(it: %a, count: Size) -> %a
@builtin(pointerSub)
pub fn retreat(it: %a, count: Size) -> %a
@builtin(pointerDifference)
pub fn difference(from: %a, to: %a) -> Size

{-
   What this compilation decided about a type - `lib/Native/Pointer.yana`'s `TypeTraits`, cut down to
   the record and the one intrinsic over it.

   **The type comes from the position, not from a value.** `traits() :: TypeTraits(I32)` asks about a
   type nothing has an instance of, which is what a `sizeOf(it: a)` cannot do at all: its parameter
   would exist only so that `a` had somewhere to come from. It binds `a` out of the result exactly as
   `load` above binds a pointee.

   **The field list is the compiler's**, in this order: `compiler/resolve_mir/metric.def` has one row
   per field and `expandTypeTraits` holds the two lists to each other by count. A sixth field here
   with no row there is reported rather than filled with whatever the fifth left behind.

   `stride` is not `size`, and it is the field this record exists for - see `advance` above.

   The two flags are **facts, never permissions**. They say what this compiler decided about `a`, so
   a program may read them to pick between two paths that do the same thing and may not read them to
   decide what it owns: a body compiled against an unconstrained `a` is checked as though both were
   the pessimistic answer whichever way they come out at one call site.
-}
pub data TypeTraits(a) {
    size: Size,
    align: Size,
    stride: Size,
    trivialCopy: Bool,
    hasTeardown: Bool
}

@builtin(typeTraits)
pub fn traits() -> TypeTraits(a)

{-
   The same question asked about a value in hand, and the three single measurements over it.

   Written here rather than supplied by the compiler, which is the point of the split: `traits` is
   the one thing the language cannot say about itself, and "the type of this expression" is something
   it says everywhere. So there is one intrinsic and the conveniences are source.
-}
pub fn traitsOf(it: a) -> TypeTraits(a) = traits()

pub fn sizeOf(it: a) -> Size = traitsOf(it).size
pub fn alignOf(it: a) -> Size = traitsOf(it).align
pub fn strideOf(it: a) -> Size = traitsOf(it).stride

{-
   The null pointer, and the test for it.

   **Written here rather than supplied by the compiler**, and that is a statement about the language
   rather than an economy: address zero is an ordinary address on bare hardware and inside a kernel,
   so "the null pointer" is a convention *this module* holds and not a fact the core language knows.
   The rung both are written over is `bitcast`, which is where an address and the word holding it
   already meet.

   `null` needs its type from context - an assignment, a return, or an ascription - which is why the
   test exists separately: `isNull(p)` needs nothing.

   `0 :: Size` and not a bare `0`, because there are two rungs to an address - `Size` and `USize` -
   and a literal with nothing to settle it would name neither.
-}
pub fn null() -> %a = bitcast(0 :: Size)
pub fn isNull(it: %a) -> Bool = (bitcast(it) :: Size) == 0
